In the context of list decoding for the rank metric, we improved an existing decoding error bound, reducing it from a factor of half-norms to the norm of the error vector alone. This more precise estimate holds when the code's structure matrix has a specific property called high minimum rank, and it assumes that the decoding list contains at most two words. Furthermore, this error bound can be achieved through a decoding strategy that produces one list containing at most two words. If the decoding list contains more than two words, we developed another error bound with a factor of half-norms. Both of these error bounds were obtained by examining a specific subset of the decoding list's rows and utilizing their linear independence to establish a relationship between their sum and the norm of the error vector. This connection can be used to determine the desired distance threshold for a given list size. Finally, it is worth noting that if the code's structure matrix has a rank of two, our decoding method reduces to the one previously proposed for that specific case. Additionally, our error decay rate under random rank metric errors matches the existing result, which was initially obtained through simulation but has now been proven theoretically. Lastly, our research demonstrated that for a random error matrix, the distance threshold can be characterized as the smallest eigenvalue of a specific random matrix multiplied by the norm of the error matrix. This finding has implications for the error decay rate under random rank metric errors. Moreover, it enables the derivation of the distance threshold for the Gaussian rank metric error scenario, which has been previously unreported. Lastly, our results can be applied to the Gaussian codebook decoding problem, which is an important issue in random beamforming. This research was conducted in partnership with the Thales Group and was supported by the French National Research Agency. It has also been registered with the CNRS 